<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="recitation-Matrix-Exponential">
  <title>Matrix Exponential</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In Calculus II, you saw the series expansion of the exponential function:
      <md>
        <mrow> e^{at} \amp = 1 + at + \frac{a^2t^2}{2!} + \frac{a^3t^3}{3!} \dotsb = \sum_{k = 0}^{\infty} \frac{a^kt^k}{k!} </mrow>
      </md>
      We can use this formula to expand the definition of <m>e^{ax}</m> to involve matrices.
      For an <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow> e^{At} \amp = 1 + at + \frac{A^2t^2}{2!} + \frac{A^3t^3}{3!} \dotsb = \sum_{k = 0}^{\infty} \frac{A^kt^k}{k!} </mrow>
      </md>
      The result of calculating <m>e^{At}</m> should be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix, and, by taking the derivative term-by-term in the series above:
      <md>
        <mrow> \frac{d}{dt}\left( e^{At}\right) = A e^{At}. </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <page>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} a \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp b \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </introduction>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Calculate <m>A^2</m>.
            Calculate <m>A^3</m>.
            Find a nice formula for <m>A^n</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Calculate <m>e^{At}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>